# Keeping a Small Team's Notes Useful

Notes only help when people can find things again. A shared notebook
that nobody searches is just a slower way to lose information. The
habits below are ordinary, but they compound: a clear title today
saves an hour of digging next quarter.

## Write the title you would search for

Titles are retrieval cues, not decoration. "Meeting notes, Tuesday"
tells a future reader almost nothing. "Vendor review: storage quotes
and renewal dates" answers the only question that matters later,
which is whether this note is the one being looked for.

Short, specific titles also make lists scannable. When every entry
begins with a date or a project name, the eye can skip whole blocks
without reading them.

## Keep one running page per project

A project accumulates decisions, links, open questions, and small
facts that are too minor for a document of their own. One running
page per project gives all of that a single home. When the page
grows past a few screens, split off the oldest section into an
archive page and link to it from the top.

The rule that makes this work is simple: if a fact belongs to a
project, it goes on that project's page first. Sorting it out later
is possible; finding it again is the hard part, and later sorting
rarely happens.

## End each week with five lines

A weekly summary does not need to be thorough. Five lines are
enough: what shipped, what is stuck, what changed, who is waiting
on whom, and what happens next week. Written while the week is
fresh, those five lines are worth more than a long report written
a month afterwards from memory.

## A short checklist

- Title every note as a search query.
- One running page per project, archived when it grows.
- Five-line summary every Friday.
- Link, do not copy, when a fact lives somewhere else.
- Delete notes that turned out to be wrong, or mark them clearly.

None of this needs special software. It needs agreement, a little
discipline, and the willingness to write the boring line now so
that nobody has to reconstruct it under pressure later.
